#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124259 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/id #uuid "a285ae3e-4f06-5e09-a06f-b2620be99888" :question/math-academy-id "e-5504" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $\\lambda_1=-1$, $\\lambda_2=1$, $\\lambda_3=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & b & 0 \\\\ a & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=0\n$$\n\nwe construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P$, we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns:\n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1}$,\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1}$, and\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=0$.\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=0$, $b=-1$, and $c=-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124260 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "3dd8d6e6-76a2-591c-90a5-86074539686b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5505" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nDetermine whether $A=PDP^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D$, where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D$." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb R$.\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A$. To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 - λ & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(4 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}2 - λ & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(4 - λ)([λ^{2} - 3λ + 2] - 12) &= 0 \\\\\n(4 - λ)(λ^{2} - 3λ - 10) &= 0 \\\\\n(4 - λ)(λ - 5)(λ + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\nSince all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124261 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "b3307ae8-eb7d-533a-aef2-c7f50dea07e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5506" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP$, find the first column of the matrix $P$." :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=2\n$$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-2\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-2\n$$\n\nComputing $A-(-2)I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λ_{1}I &= \\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{bmatrix} - (-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-\\frac{3}{2})R_{1} \\\\ R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{8}{3})R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{2}{3})R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3$.\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124303 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "8d2aff64-bf98-560b-867d-6b47ccc7dbe7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7330" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2$, $\\lambda_2=1$, and $\\lambda_3=0$.\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP$." :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix:\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=2,\\quad\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=1, \\quad\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=0\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A$. Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=2$, we get\n$A - 2I|= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_2=1$, we get\n$A - I|= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_3=0$, we get\n$A - 0I|= A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97d6e262-29a2-56f4-9f45-19519e1ccdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122777} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3146c0-67bd-5977-a9e5-b7ac3eb98c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122778} {:answer/id #uuid "cad04495-c13d-5af4-ae64-4510fd7e5a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122779} {:answer/id #uuid "f75f6779-aa2a-58cd-9014-d55bed56dae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122780} {:answer/id #uuid "6548c518-7cc8-5566-a9a4-0122eb0d4e34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cad04495-c13d-5af4-ae64-4510fd7e5a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122779} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d95111-fa09-5102-8ef4-d9b3d60d291d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122776}] :question/id #uuid "6b036f0f-ab37-53c9-a8c1-aa9fe52bf1e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37250" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 1 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that $D = P^{-1}AP$, find the first column of the matrix $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99143319-a5dc-5a1b-a641-88805ae7651f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122813} {:answer/id #uuid "c74d2f8a-ce07-5224-b401-3f2f744516fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122814} {:answer/id #uuid "f93367ea-485b-512b-9a37-9d13ff2a3f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122815} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6a6893-be6f-560b-9b8c-d18b5e7f771d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122816} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8b2e62-048a-5bdc-b7ab-39c551135f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f93367ea-485b-512b-9a37-9d13ff2a3f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122815} :answer-field/id #uuid "31f18fd6-ee4f-5f68-b7e0-5b41f1315ebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122812}] :question/id #uuid "f2deb6a7-2499-5dc5-960d-304a334f12e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37273" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & b & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $A = PDP^{-1}$, find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e98240a0-9d46-5045-bf33-10be0beaf64d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122819} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe64335-b5b3-5866-90f6-986f9b6d8dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122820} {:answer/id #uuid "3226af9e-c878-539d-8f96-63482252e2d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122821} {:answer/id #uuid "5f92e2e7-97c8-578d-946e-9983dd0cce75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122822} {:answer/id #uuid "7405ba18-dfa7-5eca-a801-2038723298ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e98240a0-9d46-5045-bf33-10be0beaf64d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122819} :answer-field/id #uuid "e70abb8d-a678-52b1-8339-66831d6cbc05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122818}] :question/id #uuid "a033518a-f4fb-5fd7-9472-bd2a3557ddc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37275" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-2$, $λ_{2} =-1$, and $λ_{3} = 1$.\nFind